<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yohl Ikʼnal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 583–604</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Lady Kan Ik, Lady Kʼanal Ikʼnal, Ix Yohl Ikʼnal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yohl Ikʼnal was queen regnant of the Maya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, ancient Lakamha, from her accession on 23 December 583 CE until her death on 7 November 604. She is the first definitively identified woman in recorded Maya history to have held full royal authority and the complete royal title of kʼuhul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaw">ajaw</a>, divine lord or lady. In 599 Palenque was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, probably on the orders of its ruler Scroll Serpent, and likely paid it tribute, but she went on ruling and was succeeded by Ajen Yohl Mat, apparently her son. Her grandson or great-grandson Kʼinich Janaab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a> I stressed his descent from her, and she is shown twice on his sarcophagus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yohl Ikʼnal</b>, also known as Lady Kan Ik and Lady Kʼanal Ikʼnal, acceded to the throne of the Maya city-state of Palenque as queen regnant.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
